"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.3 and pathological findings demonstrating pT3b disease with positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and documented lymph node metastasis. The biopsy results showed different Gleason patterns compared to the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+3 vs. 3+4).\n\nThe presence of nodal metastasis is a significant prognostic factor. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents assessment of its trajectory, which would be crucial for refining risk stratification. Further information regarding subsequent PSA levels and clinical status would revise this prognosis. The current documentation provides sufficient detail on pathological findings to guide management planning. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.09 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 33.67–50.51 months; its coverage has not been validated."
